# Architecture Rules

## Dependency Direction

Use this general dependency direction:

UI → Application → Core

Infrastructure may implement interfaces defined by Core/Application.

Core must not depend on:

- Next.js
- React
- database clients
- Clerk
- HTTP clients
- LLM providers
- browser APIs

## Layer Responsibilities

### Core

Contains:

- domain types
- ProjectSpec
- validation schemas
- renderer contracts
- adapter contracts
- deterministic generation logic
- domain-level errors

### Application

Contains:

- use cases
- orchestration
- interview workflows
- context generation workflows
- application services

### Infrastructure

Contains:

- database implementations
- authentication integrations
- LLM clients
- external APIs
- storage
- environment configuration

### UI

Contains:

- pages
- layouts
- components
- forms
- client interactions
- presentation state

## Repository Structure

Prefer a structure similar to:

```text
src/
  app/
  components/
  core/
  application/
  infrastructure/
  lib/
  types/
tests/
```

Do not create folders merely to make the repository look sophisticated.

## Change Safety

Before modifying an existing abstraction:

1. Find all usages.
2. Understand its responsibility.
3. Check whether the change belongs in its current layer.
4. Prefer the smallest compatible change.

Avoid circular dependencies and unnecessary cross-layer imports.
